WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.770
Otto Skorzeny, henchman, assassin, commando,
spy, double-agent, mercenary.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.590
What was the truth about him?

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.969
You asked for him like a million times, you
got him.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:17.320
Roll title!

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.789
The Most Dangerous Man in Vienna

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:27.660
Otto Skorzeny was born into a middle-class
family in Vienna, Austria, on June 12, 1908.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.961
As a student he distinguished himself in scientific
topics, and after graduating he enlisted at

00:00:32.961 --> 00:00:35.770
the University of Vienna as an engineering
student.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.190
His great passion was fencing.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.980
He joined the University fencing team and
during a match he received the prominent scar

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.080
on his cheek, known in German as a ‘Schmiss’
which was then a coveted mark of bravery among

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.700
German and Austrian youth.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.280
In 1931, as Nazism was gaining popularity
in Europe, Skorzeny joined the Austrian Nazi

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.700
Party, filling the ranks of the local version
of the paramilitary SA Brownshirts.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.210
Clever, ambitious and physically imposing
at 6 ft 4 [1.93 Metres] he must have been

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.430
a valued member.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.870
In the meanwhile he had earned his degree
and had started working as a civil engineer.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:16.359
The Most Dangerous Man in Berlin
Austria had become part of Germany with the

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:17.649
1938 Anschluss.

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:22.659
So, when World War II broke out in 1939, Skorzeny
applied to join the Wehrmacht.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:27.150
But his military career got off to a bumpy
start when his application to join the Luftwaffe

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.150
was denied.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.100
He was told he was too tall and too old at
the age of 31.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.789
Instead, he joined the SS and became an officer-cadet
in the Liebstandarte, Hitler’s bodyguard

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:36.789
regiment.

00:01:36.789 --> 00:01:40.659
In 1940, Skorzeny was a second lieutenant
in the Waffen-SS.

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:45.159
His engineering skills proved useful when
he designed special ramps to load tanks onto

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:46.159
ships.

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:49.670
But he also proved his courage under fire
during combat in Holland, France, and the

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:50.670
Balkans.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:55.979
Here he was decorated after capturing a large
Yugoslav force, and was promoted to first

00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:56.979
lieutenant.

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:00.740
Skorzeny was then transferred to the Eastern
Front after the launch of Operation Barbarossa

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.590
in June 1941, with the 2nd SS Panzer Division
“Das Reich”.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:11.090
He was part of the unsuccessful siege of Moscow
and in December 1942, now a captain, received

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.440
a head wound from a piece of shrapnel.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.920
Skorzeny was awarded the Iron Cross for bravery,
and then sent home to Vienna to recuperate.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.629
While there, he became fascinated by the idea
of commando operations and read all the books

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:24.629
he could find about them.

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:29.190
He then transformed those ideas into plans
for unconventional warfare, which he submitted

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.680
to higher headquarters.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.360
And they started to take notice.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.480
His concepts soon reached the desk of Ernst
Kaltenbrunner, the new head of the Reich security

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.290
services and successor to Reinhard Heydrich.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.430
Skorzeny’s ideas were passed on to General
Schellenberg, head of the SS foreign intelligence

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.439
service.

00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:48.569
Skorzeny and Schellenberg met and the General
was so impressed that he appointed him as

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:52.310
commander of the newly created Waffen Sonderverband
z.b.V.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.569
Friedenthal, an SS unit dedicated to special
operations.

00:02:56.569 --> 00:02:59.030
His career as a commando leader had just begun.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:07.340
The Most Dangerous Man on a Glider
Skorzeny and his unit launched into their

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.489
first mission in the summer of 1943: Operation
François.

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:15.599
The plan was to organize the nomadic Qashqai
people, in Iran, into an armed guerrilla force

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:17.939
which could serve the German war effort.

00:03:17.939 --> 00:03:22.010
Skorzeny’s paratroopers were also assigned
to disrupt the supply lines between the Allies

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.980
and the Soviets and to turn the local population
against the Allied presence.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.090
Skorzeny parachuted into Northern Iran packed
with gold and explosives.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.069
His intention was to bribe the tribesmen elders
and win their support for the mobilization

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:35.420
of the entire people.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:40.390
The operation proved to be a failure: a fellow
agent, Paul Ernst Fackenheim, made a remark

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.640
that as soon as they were out of gold, the
Persians sold them to the British.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:49.030
Skorzeny’s next mission would prove his
most famous and spectacular success: Operation

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:55.510
Oak, the rescue of Benito Mussolini, imprisoned
after the Monarchist coup in July 1943.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.400
First Skorzeny had to find Mussolini.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.620
The ex-dictator was continually moved from
one hiding place to another, but the Germans

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.709
discovered him at a villa on the isle of La
Maddalena, near Sardinia.

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:10.240
Skorzeny then flew over in a Heinkel He-111
bomber to take aerial photos of the location,

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.980
but the plane was shot down by Allied fighters
and crashed into the sea.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.970
Skorzeny and his men were rescued by an Italian
warship.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.560
Mussolini was moved again and the chase continued.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.280
Finally, Skorzeny tracked him down to the
Campo Imperatore Hotel, a remote and fortified

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.960
resort on the Gran Sasso mountain in central
Italy.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.330
The hotel was accessible only by funicular.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:35.280
He, Luftwaffe General Kurt Student and Major
Otto-Harald Mors, a paratrooper battalion

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.550
commander, came up with a workable plan.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:41.290
Skorzeny assembled a team of 107 commandos
who would be landed in gliders.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.500
On the 12th of September 1943 the gliders
approached the hotel.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:49.870
Skorzeny realised too late that what he thought
to be a patch of grass was a rocky incline.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.440
His glider almost crashed, but he made it
out in one piece.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.160
Twelve minutes later, Skorzeny had found Mussolini
and not one person had been killed.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.410
The Fuhrer, delighted with Skorzeny, awarded
him the Knights Cross.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:05.100
As a result, he became Hitler’s favourite
commando and was dubbed “the most dangerous

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.389
man in Europe” by the Allies … although
propaganda chief Dr Goebbels may have been

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:11.460
the first to give him that title.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.949
Propaganda coverage of the event however hid
the fact that most of the planning and execution

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:19.450
had to be credited to Skorzeny’s associates,
General Student and Major Mors.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:23.599
Skorzeny stole their thunder by making sure
he would be the one escorting Mussolini to

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:26.610
the get-away plane in full view of the cameras.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.360
Skorzeny though should be recognised with
the stroke of genius that ensured the raid

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.360
was bloodless.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.360
He had secured the cooperation of General
Soleti of the Carabinieri, the military police.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.120
Soleti was actually the first one to approach
the Hotel and ordered the heavily armed military

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.590
policemen to stand down.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.919
It should be noted that footage and photos
of the event show many of the Italian guards

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:50.220
posing with big smiles by Mussolini and Skorzeny,
hinting at their true loyalties.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.120
Based on testimonies from one of the guards,
Mussolini seemed to be the least happy to

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.229
have been rescued …
The next mission for Skorzeny was Operation

00:05:57.229 --> 00:05:59.600
Long Jump, November 1943.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.360
Its ambitious goal was to kill or kidnap the
Allies’ “Big Three” leaders - Stalin,

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:09.550
Churchill, Roosevelt—at their strategic
conference in Tehran, Iran.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:13.879
A first group of six German operatives landed
in Qom, before proceeding to Teheran on camel

00:06:13.879 --> 00:06:14.879
back.

00:06:14.879 --> 00:06:17.900
Skorzeny was supposed to join the group with
a second team of assassins.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.490
Unfortunately for them, a few months earlier,
the Operation’s planner SS officer von Ortel

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.960
had just made friends with a Wehrmacht Lieutenant
in a bar in Copenhagen.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.750
Blind drunk, Ortel had spilled the beans on
Long Jump.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:32.350
That lieutenant, well he was a Soviet intelligence
officer in disguise.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:36.940
Roosevelt and Churchill were alerted to the
plot and were kept safe at the Soviet Embassy.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:41.680
The NKVD then seized the six German commandos
and forced them to radio to Skorzeny's team

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.259
that the operation had failed.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:46.410
To give the full picture, some sources claim
that this plot never existed.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:51.379
It was just a clever ruse of Stalin and/or
the NKVD to force the Western leaders to stay

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:54.979
inside the Soviet Embassy, where their conversations
could be easily bugged.

00:06:54.979 --> 00:06:59.790
The next high level hit commissioned to Skorzeny
was the capture of Tito, Operation Knight’s

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:00.790
Leap.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.930
Also in this case Skorzeny and his commandos
attacked by gliders, while conventional forces

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.949
engaged Tito’s partisans around his mountain
helicopters.

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:08.800
Did I say helicopters?

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.660
Sorry, I meant headquarters.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.900
In any case, the partisans’ resistance was
fierce, stalling the pincer movement and allowing

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.370
Tito to escape.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:20.580
Skorzeny would finally have time to shine
after the 20th of July 1944, following the

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.020
attempted bombing on Hitler.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:27.190
In his memoir, he claimed he played an integral
part in restoring order to Berlin, where the

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:31.099
conspirators had initiated plan ‘Valkyrie’
to topple the regime.

00:07:31.099 --> 00:07:35.700
Skorzeny infiltrated the conspirators’ base
of operations and had the “Valkyrie” order

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:36.700
rescinded.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:41.449
This was an order intended to quell a possible
coup, but had been cleverly exploited by the

00:07:41.449 --> 00:07:45.650
conspirators to trick German troops into arresting
loyal Nazi officials.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:50.729
Skorzeny’s actions contributed to dissipate
confusion, restore communications to Fuhrer

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:54.860
Headquarters and preventing a possible civil
war between German troops.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.909
Skorzeny took charge of Wehrmacht administration
until normalcy returned.

00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:01.250
Again, Hitler was delighted.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:06.871
In October 1944 he dispatched him to Budapest
to lead Operation Panzerfaust, aka Operation

00:08:06.871 --> 00:08:08.229
Mickey Mouse.

00:08:08.229 --> 00:08:13.500
The Fuehrer was not pleased with Hungarian
leader Admiral Horthy, an uneasy ally against

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.530
the Soviet Union, who was actually pro-American
and had always refused to deport the Jewish

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:19.530
population.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:24.139
The Germans got wind that he was negotiating
with the allies and Skorzeny was sent to exact

00:08:24.139 --> 00:08:25.169
punishment.

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:29.220
Skorzeny and his commandos stormed the presidential
palace, seized the Admiral’s son, rolled

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.370
him into a carpet and kidnapped him.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:35.030
Horthy was forced to negotiate, and eventually
to stand down.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:41.000
His leadership was replaced by a puppet government
under the Arrow Cross party, staunchly anti-Semitic.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:47.220
During the following 56 days, an estimated
320,000 Hungarian Jews were sent to their

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.750
deaths in the gas chambers.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.240
Another 100,000 starved or died of disease.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.290
In December of 1944 Skorzeny launched his
most infamous mission: Operation Greif, or

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:58.290
‘Griffin’.

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.590
The goal was to capture key bridges over the
Meuse river during the Battle of the Bulge,

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.610
the last German offensive in the West.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:07.470
Skorzeny’s plan was ingenious: he selected
commandos who were fluent in English, dressed

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:12.800
them in American uniforms and sent them behind
enemy lines in Belgium to spread panic and

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:13.800
confusion.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.839
Skorzeny’s men cut communication wires,
issued fake orders, and turned around road

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:18.839
signs.

00:09:18.839 --> 00:09:22.010
The Americans grew paranoid: some GIs fired
on each other, while others grilled their

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:25.339
mates about American popular culture to identify
if they were German agents.

00:09:25.339 --> 00:09:30.750
If you were American, but did not care about
baseball nor movie stars, you could get arrested!

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:36.190
At one point, Field Marshal Bernard Montgomery
refused to show his ID and had his car tires

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:37.200
shot out.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.760
He was then dragged into a barn and restrained
until his identity could be confirmed.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.100
Skorzeny spread the false rumour among his
men that the real aim was the assassination

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:47.860
of General Eisenhower, headquartered in Paris.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.910
German agents captured by the Americans, confessed
to this plot.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:54.950
As a result, Eisenhower was put under virtual
arrest for his own protection.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:59.490
Griffin eventually failed and many Germans
were easily identified and shot as spies.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:04.880
As the Reich crumbled, Skorzeny’s final
involvement was with Operation Werewolf.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:08.730
This was a resistance movement, mostly composed
of Hitler Youth members, trained in guerrilla

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:11.310
tactics to oppose an Allied occupation.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:15.450
It actually was a desperate propaganda ruse
by Goebbels to raise morale.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:20.600
But Skorzeny hijacked the plan and used this
small force to aid the escaping Nazi high

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:21.600
officials.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.930
The Soviet NKVD, probably overestimating the
size and importance of Werewolf, killed roughly

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:30.440
5,000 boys, aged 15 to 17, for suspected guerrilla
activity.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.580
A few days after Hitler’s suicide, Skorzeny
handed himself over to the Americans.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:39.080
He was a highly decorated, well-respected
military professional but his directive for

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:42.250
his men to wear American uniforms got him
in trouble.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.839
This was considered a war crime and was put
on trial in 1947.

00:10:45.839 --> 00:10:50.220
Luckily for him, he escaped execution when
British SOE operatives confirmed they wore

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.880
German uniforms during the war.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.240
But he had to answer from other charges.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.050
Before he could be prosecuted, though, Skorzeny
escaped from the POW camp with the help of

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.700
two ex-SS men, dressed as American military
police.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:07.080
He later claimed this was a plan of the OSS,
the CIA’s predecessor: his freedom, in return

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:08.200
for his services.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.830
Was this true?

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:18.390
The Most Dangerous Man in Madrid
Skorzeny first hid in Bavaria, then Salzburg

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:22.980
and finally Spain, where he cooperated with
Francisco Franco’s military and intelligence

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:23.980
services.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:28.420
He was “de-Nazified” in absentia by a
German court in 1952 but was still a “person

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:31.880
of interest” for the famed Austrian Nazi
hunter, Simon Wiesenthal.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.320
In any case, he could lead an open life.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.580
He had a young wife, Ilse von Finckenstein.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:40.360
He had several legitimate activities: an engineering
firm, an import-export business … and a

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:41.700
mercenary security company.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.920
But he apparently had time for other endeavours.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:49.680
He founded a Neo-Nazi group called CEDADE,
the Spanish Circle of Friends of Europe and

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.050
is rumoured to have smuggled ex-Nazis to South
America.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:57.829
Particularly in Argentina, where he served
briefly as military advisor to Juan Perón.

00:11:57.829 --> 00:12:01.930
Most notably he was bodyguard to Evita.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:03.260
There are even rumours of an affair between
the two.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:06.620
I wonder who may have spread them …
In the late 50s he became fixated with gaining

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.040
entry to the UK.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:13.339
This may be the reason why in 1959 Otto Skorzeny
bought a farm in County Kildare, Ireland.

00:12:13.339 --> 00:12:17.860
He was granted temporary visas to stay in
Ireland, but state records mention his indignation

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:22.520
at the continual refusal of the British authorities
to allow him entry in the UK.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:26.660
Newspaper reports in the 1960s give another
version: his farm in Ireland was a place where

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:31.140
fleeing Nazis could hide, but no evidence
was found to substantiate this claim.

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:36.190
Maybe he just liked the Guinness?

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:41.220
The Most Dangerous Man in Tel Aviv
And now for something truly puzzling.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:45.790
As an escaped former SS, who had indirectly
contributed to the deportation of the Hungarian

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:51.280
Jews, Skorzeny knew very well that his name
was top in the list of Nazi hunter Simon Wiesenthal.

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:57.120
And so, he was the first one to be surprised
when agents of the Mossad, Israeli intelligence,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.190
came to recruit him …
Early 1962.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.870
Skorzeny was having some drinks at a bar in
Madrid with his wife Ilse.

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:06.829
The two befriended another younger couple
of German tourists, who had just been robbed

00:13:06.829 --> 00:13:08.270
and were looking for some help.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:12.420
The two couples went on drinking and, apparently,
flirting, until the Skorzenys invited them

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:13.420
to their villa.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:17.390
Just when things seemed to, well … kick
off … well they did kick off, but in another

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:18.390
way.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:19.757
Skorzeny pulled a gun on the couple:
“I know who you are, and I know why you

00:13:19.757 --> 00:13:20.757
are here.

00:13:20.757 --> 00:13:23.510
You are Mossad, and you’ve come here to
kill me!”

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.320
The man replied
“If we had come to kill you, you would have

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.560
been dead weeks ago.”

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:31.589
The two operatives were there to offer Skorzeny
a deal: they needed his services and would

00:13:31.589 --> 00:13:32.750
pay handsomely.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.700
Skorzeny’s was not interested in money.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:38.300
What he wanted, was to be removed from Wiesenthal’s
list.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:40.589
The Mossad agents agreed.

00:13:40.589 --> 00:13:44.360
Skorzeny was flown to Tel Aviv, where he met
Mossad agent Joe Ranaan.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:48.269
He was also Viennese, but as a Jew, he had
lost all his family in the camps.

00:13:48.269 --> 00:13:52.509
Ranaan took the ex-SS to the Yad Vashem, the
Holocaust Museum, and observed that he appeared

00:13:52.509 --> 00:13:53.509
respectful.

00:13:53.509 --> 00:13:58.120
Skorzeny’s mission was to thwart Egypt’s
military rocket programme, led by Heinz Krug,

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.750
a German scientist formerly associated with
Werner Von Braun.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:06.000
The Austrian commando was shown a letter from
Wiesenthal, accepting the removal of his name

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.440
from the list of Nazi criminals.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.889
In truth, Wiesenthal had refused.

00:14:09.889 --> 00:14:12.279
The letter was a Mossad forgery.

00:14:12.279 --> 00:14:14.860
Skorzeny kept his promise and he delivered,
big time.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:18.930
He flew to Egypt and identified all German
scientists working for the programme.

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:23.050
He then unmasked several companies around
Europe that were secretly selling rocket components

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.050
to the Egyptians.

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:28.790
He even carried out a campaign of intimidation
against the scientists and mailed explosive

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:32.820
packages prepared by the Mossad, killing five
Egyptian technicians.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.810
One day in 1962, while in Madrid, Skorzeny
received a phone call.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.949
It was Heinz Krug, the lead scientist of the
programme.

00:14:38.949 --> 00:14:42.250
Krug was dead scared and was seeking Skorzeny’s
help and protection.

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:44.440
Skorzeny arranged to meet him in Munich.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.990
As he drove him out of town in a white Mercedes
he reassured him: he had already hired some

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:51.520
bodyguards and they were driving to meet them.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:55.190
As they stepped out of the car and into the
woods, Skorzeny shot Krug.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:59.089
Three Mossad agents then took care of the
body by dissolving it in acid.

00:14:59.089 --> 00:15:00.399
Skorzeny’s job was done.

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:02.089
He had not made a penny out of it.

00:15:02.089 --> 00:15:04.930
He didn’t know it but was still on Wiesenthal
list.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:08.420
But at least he would be spared by the Mossad.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:16.800
The Most Dangerous Man in a FOIA Search Engine
Another intelligence service famously associated

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.079
with Otto Skorzeny was the CIA.

00:15:19.079 --> 00:15:22.510
We wanted to verify this claim directly at
the source and so we did some rummaging in

00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:25.240
the CIA’s own Freedom Of Information Act
(FOIA) search engine.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:30.230
A brief memo states that in 1961 Skorzeny
had a plan to kidnap Fidel Castro.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:35.250
This plan had the approval of CIA Director
Allen Dulles, but had been vetoed by JFK.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:39.480
There is a catch: the memo quotes an interview
released by Skorzeny to a Peruvian newspaper

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:40.480
in 1966.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:43.329
Knowing the man’s knack for self-publicity,
can we truly believe this?

00:15:43.329 --> 00:15:44.329
So, we dug deeper.

00:15:44.329 --> 00:15:49.940
On the 26th of January 1951 a report was sent
to the Secretary of State from Germany by

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:54.230
John J. McCloy, American High Commissioner
for Occupied Germany.

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:58.600
McCloy reports that Chancellor Adenauer was
worried about rumours of Skorzeny training

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:01.480
the Spanish military with US covert support.

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:04.360
McCloy was not aware of this scheme and admitted
it was worrying.

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:07.380
The CIA knew very well that Skorzeny was in
Spain.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:12.600
They learned this from a letter dated 21st
of September 1949, sent by FBI Director J.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:13.600
Edgar Hoover.

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:16.259
Skorzeny had been sighted
“working in Spain with a group of Nazis

00:16:16.259 --> 00:16:17.810
with the blessings of Franco”.

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:22.240
In April 1951, the CIA was keeping tabs on
Skorzeny’s activities in Madrid.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:27.130
In particular, they wrote about internal feuding
amongst former Nazi officers, with concerns

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:28.339
for it to go public.

00:16:28.339 --> 00:16:31.100
The memo reads:
“View possibility unfavourable publicity

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.690
break as result Skorzeny’s manoeuvring.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.639
Consider important we clarify our interests
and any US connections fully.

00:16:37.639 --> 00:16:42.110
Can you explain claims Skorzeny connected
with or supported by US Colonel Thompson or

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:43.110
Stimpson.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:46.920
View categorical denial”
The agent asks the question: is possibly the

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:50.210
CIC, Counter-Intelligence Corps behind Skorzeny?

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:53.920
The CIC was a Cold War era secret service
within the US Army ranks.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:58.950
Moving to the 1960s, the CIA kept a close
eye on Skorzeny’s relationship to ‘UPHILL’

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:01.260
which appears to be a code name for an organisation.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:05.770
One report states that the Chief of Uphill
had warned that US support for Skorzeny would

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:08.070
be a source of embarrassment for the White
House.

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:10.450
This is similar to Adenauer’s warning.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:16.080
The picture that emerges so far is that some
US agency, possibly the CIC, had some form

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:20.250
of contact with him, and German authorities
had warned the US to be careful about it.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:23.560
But what of Skorzeny’s relationship with
other powers?

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.510
There is another document that complicates
matters even more.

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:28.950
The agent signs himself as ‘Heckenschuetze’
- ‘sniper’.

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:32.590
We’ll post the link in the description,
so you can read it in full, but here is the

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:33.590
gist of it.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:37.880
At the end of 1943, sensing that the War would
be lost, Martin Bormann, Hitler’s secretary,

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:40.250
created a secret organization called
‘HACKE’

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:43.580
It was so exclusive to count only 35 members.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.430
And so secretive that even Himmler was unaware
of it.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:50.870
The purpose of Hacke was to guarantee the
survival of Nazi officials in case of defeat.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:55.460
To do so, Hacke racked up half a billion dollars
from concentration camp victims and created

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:59.150
hideouts in Spain, Portugal, Italy, Japan
and Argentina.

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:02.920
But the twist is that the Soviet intelligence
had infiltrated this network, apparently by

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:07.350
blackmailing Bormann, lest they revealed the
existence of Hacke to Himmler.

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:10.310
Hacke remained active after the war, but here
comes anothet twist:

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:14.500
“One of the dark and dangerous KGB figures
in Hacke is the famous

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:18.640
SS Colonel Skorzeny
who presently lives in Madrid and also works

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.960
for the Spanish IS (intelligence service).

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:25.010
Skorzeny was under active development by Abakumow
as early as 1942 …

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:29.290
Skorzeny was finally recruited as a collaborator
of Abakumow in the middle of 1944”

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:32.570
Abakumow being a Soviet intelligence General.

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:37.070
His plan was to exploit ex-Nazi officials
and agents settled in the Americas.

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:41.310
The author of the document to be fair states
that it is unclear whether Abakumow succeeded

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:46.070
in his plans, and looks for other clues to
confirm Skorzeny’s allegiance to the KGB.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:50.500
One of these clues is that Skorzeny was very
close to another ex-Nazi operative, Karl Rudl.

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:54.820
The two had been tasked with a secret mission
by the Vatican (!!!!!) to smuggle a Hungarian

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:56.350
Cardinal out of prison.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:19:01.800
Rudl was actually an agent of Abakumow, who
had intentions to hijack the prison escape

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.530
to have the Cardinal shot.

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:07.830
Rudl smelled a rat – he was probably going
to get shot, too, while Abakumow would leave

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:08.990
Skorzeny alive.

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:11.830
And so, he aborted the operation.

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:16.200
To recap, from the 1940s to the 1960s Otto
Skorzeny was

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:20.590
A commando for the Nazi security services
A possible double-agent for the Soviets

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:25.300
A member of the neo-Nazi ring ‘HACKE’
An agent of Franco’s intelligence

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:29.780
A hitman for the Mossad
A possible informant to the US CIC

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:34.100
An operative for the Vatican
As you can see, our initial question ‘did

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.680
Skorzeny work for the CIA?’

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:37.280
seems irrelevant now.

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:39.010
The answer is probably, no.

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:43.590
If anything, his calendar was too full to
fit them in.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:52.050
The Most Dangerous Man is no more
In his later years, Otto Skorzeny fell ill

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:53.410
from lung cancer.

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:57.220
On July 5, 1975 he died in Madrid at the age
of 67.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:20:00.880
He had two funerals, one in Madrid, and the
other at his family plot in Vienna.

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:06.000
At both, he received full Nazi honours, with
veterans giving him the Nazi salute and singing

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.660
some of Hitler’s favourite songs.

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:09.790
Among those present, one man stood apart.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:15.220
He did not join in with the Nazi nostalgic
paraphernalia, but offered a silent salute

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:17.820
to a fellow secret agent and fighter.

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:21.050
Someone he had grown up hating, but had learned
to respect.

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:24.350
That man was Mossad agent Joe Ranaan.

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:29.820
And it’s with this strange image of an Israeli
spy at a Nazi funeral that we end our story

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:30.820
today.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:35.430
An image which sums up the career of a man
who had been clearly a follower of Nazi ideology

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.210
– but was ultimately loyal only to himself.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:41.550
I’d like to propose a little game for the
comments section: have a look at the CIA FOIA

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:44.590
Search page, the link is below, and have a
search around, maybe for a famous personality

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:46.710
or event, or even for your own name, why not?

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.600
And let us know what you found out!

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:54.610
As usual, thank you for watching.

